VulkanOptics (optics::)
======================

The compositing pass has two disjoint paths:
- Glass: fast screen-space refraction sampling a linearly filtered PCloud
  HDR image, with Snell-derived bounded distortion, Fresnel and mild
  thickness-dependent tint attenuation. No per-pixel BVH, no direct-light
  highlight injection, no temporal filter.
- Mirrors: full deterministic Whitted-style BVH reflections, using PCloud
  probes for indirect lighting at offscreen hits.

The host may supply an empty surface scene for glass-only compositions;
the optics compositor then has no reflection BVH to build. In scenes
containing mirrors, the shared BVH includes all scene geometry so mirrors
can continue to reflect off-camera objects. Glass and mirror surfaces are
resolved in separate fullscreen-scissor draws, each discarding pixels
that do not belong to its material.

Glass is a performance-oriented approximation: it is NOT complete
path-traced volume refraction. Its bounded screen-space distortion avoids
the strong inversion and very high GPU costs of full-resolution
multi-bounce sphere tracing. Missing offscreen background data and
accurate caustics remain limitations. Thin glass keeps zero displacement,
while solid glass has controlled distortion. Point-light glints are not
injected; mirror quality is unchanged.

BVH topology uses VulkanBVH stackless, per-swapchain skinned refits,
PCloud probes and host-owned G-buffers. The standalone VulkanPMap is
not linked into VulkanOptics.

Performance refinement: the host can supply separate conservative
scissor rectangles for glass and mirror. The original shared-region
record overload remains available for other host projects.

For a single planar mirror, the game renders a reflected camera view and
shares its plane, center and extents through the existing optics camera
push constants. The glass pass checks transmitted rays against that plane
and the opaque depth buffer, then samples the existing reflected LDR image.
This covers the planar mirror behind glass without any per-glass-pixel BVH
queries. Non-planar or multiple simultaneous mirrors remain subject to the
cheap glass compositor's screen-space limitations.

Glass-through-planar-mirror correction: glass projects a transmitted
mirror hit into the actual reflected camera (rather than horizontally
flipping primary-camera UVs), samples the reflected HDR image and applies
the shared tone mapper once. The ordinary mirror keeps its existing LDR
projection and shading. The additional reflection_hdr input is optional
for hosts that do not require this glass/mirror combination.

Glass refraction through a planar mirror now uses the **same tone-mapped
reflection image and screen-space horizontal sampling convention as the
ordinary planar mirror shader**. Analytic mirror-plane intersection only
selects the already-visible mirror region behind the glass; bounded UV
refraction shifts the sample slightly but does not magnify or invert it.
The unused reflected-HDR descriptor and PCloud HDR-view accessor were
removed. This is a fast single-interface approximation, not volumetric
ray tracing, and nonplanar/multiple-mirror transmission remains limited.

## Bounded optical layers
The game may provide a second depth-peeled optical G-buffer for two
front-facing glass surfaces in the primary camera, and an optical G-buffer
for glass in the planar reflected camera. These are optional integration
features of the host's rendering architecture; they add raster work but
no per-glass-pixel BVH traversal. Normal scene transmission and mirror
transmission share the same glass color function. The second primary
layer is ignored behind the mirror plane; glass visible *in* the mirror
comes from the reflected camera's optical G-buffer.

Currently there are at most two primary glass layers and one reflected
glass layer. Unlimited recursion and glass through secondary nonplanar
mirrors are not provided by this fast path.
